B.OO=new A.b4("confirmshaming","Confirmshaming","Phrases like 'No, I hate saving money' exploit social norms and self-image to nudge acceptance of newsletters or tracking.","Harry Brignull (2010)","Ignore the shaming language and make decisions based on your actual preferences; use email aliases.",B.cj,B.pR,"","Academic audits of digital dark patterns indicate that guilt-driven confirmshaming is present in over 11% of the top e-commerce platforms.",!1)
B.R0=new A.a5(61888,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OF=new A.b4("roach_motel","Roach Motel","Designs make entry easy while hiding or complicating exit through buried options, forced calls, or multi-step processes.","Harry Brignull (2010)","Before signing up, check how to cancel. Use app-store billing where cancellation is easier and centralized.",B.cj,B.R0,"","Consumer protection audits reveal that while it takes an average of 1.5 clicks to subscribe, it takes 6 or more clicks across multiple buried screens to cancel.",!1)
B.QW=new A.a5(61711,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OT=new A.b4("zeigarnik_effect","Zeigarnik Effect","People remember and feel tension about incomplete tasks more than completed ones. Interfaces use progress bars to nudge users to complete onboarding or upsell flows.","Bluma Zeigarnik (1927)","Treat 'X% complete' as a persuasive cue, not a duty. Pause and ask whether the remaining steps benefit you or mainly the platform.",B.pu,B.QW,"","Psychological studies show that humans are up to 90% more likely to remember and fixate on an incomplete task compared to a finished one.",!1)
B.c1=new A.hg("Ad Psychology",10,"adPsychology")
B.R1=new A.a5(61917,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pd=new A.b4("rewarded_ads","Bribed Attention","Converts a commercial into a 'mission' that feels like part of the game loop, making the ad feel like progress rather than an interruption.","Mobile Gaming Industry (Early 2010s)","Only watch rewarded ads for games you truly enjoy and set a strict daily limit (e.g., 'max 3 ads') so advertisers do not control your playtime.",B.c1,B.R1,"Academic reviews of ad monetization in children's applications.","Analyses note rewarded ads heavily boost engagement because children focus on the immediate reward and often fail to recognize the persuasive intent.",!0)
B.QM=new A.a5(59050,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pe=new A.b4("advergames","Advergames","Builds an entire game around a brand, ensuring your fun and positive feelings transfer directly onto the product.","Branded Web Games (2000s)","Ask yourself: 'Would I still play this if the brand logo was removed?' If no, limit your time with it.",B.c1,B.QM,"Research on children and advergames.","Children who play an advergame for a snack show significantly higher purchase intentions if they enjoy the game.",!0)
B.P1=new A.b4("in_game_placement","In-Game Placements","Embeds real-world brands into the game environment so you become accustomed to seeing them in fun and engaging contexts.","3D Online Gaming (2000s)","Spot the brands and ask: 'Is this here to make the world realistic, or did someone pay for this space?'",B.c1,B.pS,"Experiments comparing in-game ads and advergames.","In-game ads raise purchase intentions by integrating brands seamlessly into enjoyable play.",!0)
B.Ph=new A.b4("brand_mascots","Brand Mascots","Uses cute or human-like NPCs to trick your brain into treating them as trustworthy friends rather than corporate sales tools.","Classic Advertising to Digital NPCs","When a cute character recommends a purchase, picture the faceless corporate logo saying it instead to break the illusion.",B.c1,B.pQ,"Studies on brand anthropomorphism.","Giving brands human-like traits measurably increases perceived warmth and trust, boosting positive evaluations.",!0)
B.QJ=new A.a5(58978,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OS=new A.b4("scarcity_timers","Scarcity Timers","Leverages the fear of missing out with fake deadlines or low stock warnings to push impulsive buying over careful thinking.","Retail Flash Sales to E-commerce","Never buy under a countdown. Take a screenshot, walk away, and wait 24 hours before deciding.",B.c1,B.QJ,"Behavioral psychology of scarcity.","Scarcity messages increase urgency and willingness to pay, heavily influencing targeted personality types.",!0)
B.Qt=new A.a5(58502,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.P2=new A.b4("social_proof_ads","Weaponized Social Proof","Claims a product is highly popular to make you assume it must be good and blindly copy the crowd.","E-commerce Ratings & Reviews","Ignore the popularity label entirely. Ask: 'Does this actually improve my experience for the price?'",B.c1,B.Qt,"Marketing studies on social proof.","Scarcity combined with social proof ('only 2 left, 1,500 bought') significantly speeds up checkout decisions.",!0)
B.QN=new A.a5(59070,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.P6=new A.b4("native_ads","Stealth Native Ads","Designs ads to look exactly like regular posts or streams so your critical guard stays completely down.","Social Media Platforms (2010s)","Habitually scan for '#ad' or 'sponsored' tags, and always ask 'Who paid for this?' when watching influencers.",B.c1,B.QN,"Studies on ad recognition and disclosure.","Only 17\u201327% of users correctly identify native ads as advertising, even with generic disclosure labels.",!0)
B.OQ=new A.b4("subliminal_priming","Subliminal Priming","Displays brief or subtle brand cues when you are vulnerable to secretly nudge your later choices.","Classic Lab Studies to Modern UI","When you suddenly crave a brand, trace it back. Did you just see it in a game? Naming the influence kills its power.",B.c1,B.lw,"Neuroimaging work on dynamic food ads.","Dynamic brand ads activate brain reward regions more strongly than static images, bypassing conscious filtering.",!0)
B.PY=new A.a5(57578,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pn=new A.b4("halo_effect","The Halo Effect","Relies on a positive experience with one product to make you unconsciously assume everything else with that logo is equally good.","Classic Psychology","Evaluate every purchase on its own merits. Pretend the item has no brand name and check its actual features.",B.c1,B.PY,"Marketing research on brand ecosystems.","A strong flagship product creates a psychological 'halo' that improves loyalty across entire brand ecosystems.",!0)
B.PX=new A.a5(57567,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OE=new A.b4("neuromarketing","Neuromarketing","Advertisers use brain scans to find exactly which colors and sounds light up your reward circuits the most.","Biometric Ad Firms (2000s)","Feeling moved by an ad doesn't mean it's a good deal. Pause and check objective factors like price and utility.",B.c1,B.PX,"Reviews of fMRI ad optimization.","Emotionally rich ads reliably activate the amygdala and insula, correlating directly with increased purchasing behavior.",!0)
B.Ra=new A.a5(62526,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OZ=new A.b4("like_button","The Like Button","Skinner's variable ratio schedule applied to social validation. You never know how many likes you will get, making you check repeatedly.","Facebook (2009)","Use browser extensions to hide metrics, or disable notifications for likes.",B.lp,B.Ra,"",null,!1)
B.OC=new A.b4("loot_boxes","Loot Boxes","Classified as gambling in several countries; exploits developing brains by linking real money to randomized digital rewards.","Online Gaming Industry","Ban purchases of loot boxes or use parental controls to prevent in-app purchases.",B.f6,B.hL,"",null,!1)
B.QQ=new A.a5(61187,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OH=new A.b4("filter_bubble","Filter Bubble","Algorithms silently narrowing worldviews by only showing content that aligns with your past behavior, creating an ideological echo chamber.","Eli Pariser (2011)","Actively seek out diverse viewpoints and use chronological feeds when possible.",B.hD,B.QQ,"",null,!1)
B.R9=new A.a5(62465,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.P8=new A.b4("parasocial_relationships","Parasocial Relationships","Exploiting one-sided emotional attachments to influencers to sell products or keep you engaged on the platform.","Social Media Influencers","Remember that influencers are playing a character to monetize your attention.",B.dY,B.R9,"",null,!1)
B.QI=new A.a5(58928,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OM=new A.b4("reciprocity_triggers","Reciprocity Triggers","Manufacturing social obligation via follow-back notifications, making you feel guilty if you don't return the favor.","Social Networking Dynamics","Recognize that digital follows are not social debts. Only follow accounts you actually care about.",B.dY,B.QI,"",null,!1)
B.R4=new A.a5(62160,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OY=new A.b4("privacy_zuckering","Privacy Zuckering","Deceptive UI tricking users into sharing unintended data by making privacy settings confusing or misleading.","Tim Jones (2010)","Take time to review privacy settings instead of clicking 'Accept All'.",B.cj,B.R4,"",null,!1)
B.R2=new A.a5(61941,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OK=new A.b4("cliffhanger_engineering","Cliffhanger Engineering","Episodes mathematically structured to end mid-tension, forcing you to watch the next episode to resolve the psychological loop.","Streaming Platforms","Stop watching an episode in the middle of a scene rather than waiting for the end credits.",B.lq,B.R2,"",null,!1)
B.PW=new A.a5(57537,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pm=new A.b4("frictionless_autoplay","Frictionless Autoplay","Removing the decision gap between content to enforce bingeing. The next video starts before you even realize you're watching it.","Netflix / YouTube","Turn off autoplay in your account settings.",B.lq,B.PW,"",null,!1)
B.QC=new A.a5(58710,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pl=new A.b4("watch_time_optimisation","Watch Time Optimisation","Rewarding creators for duration over quality, resulting in padded content that artificially inflates your screen time.","Video Platform Algorithms","Use playback speed controls or skip ahead if content feels unnecessarily drawn out.",B.hD,B.QC,"",null,!1)
B.Q8=new A.a5(57696,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OX=new A.b4("age_ambiguity","Age Ambiguity","Platforms designed to be addictive before age verification kicks in, hooking young users early.","Social Media / Gaming","Use strict parental controls and monitor account creation.",B.cj,B.Q8,"",null,!1)
B.QF=new A.a5(58875,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.P5=new A.b4("kid_targeted_variable_rewards","Kid-targeted Variable Rewards","Using flashy animations and unpredictable digital rewards to hook children whose impulse control is not fully developed.","Youth Gaming","Limit exposure to games with randomized reward mechanisms for minors.",B.f6,B.QF,"",null,!1)
B.OG=new A.b4("premium_currency_confusion","Premium Currency Confusion","Using an intermediate digital currency to obscure the real-world cost of in-game items, making spending feel 'free'.","Free-to-Play Games","Mentally convert all in-game currency prices to real dollars before purchasing.",B.cj,B.lv,"",null,!1)
B.QR=new A.a5(61201,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OW=new A.b4("daily_login_rewards","Daily Login Rewards","Escalating login bonuses make missing a day feel like losing accumulated value, turning 'just checking in' into a high-pressure habit.","Free-to-Play Game Design (2010s).","Disable streak-style rewards where possible, or decide specific play days and accept that virtual bonuses may reset outside that schedule.",B.f6,B.QR,"","Analyses of live-service games repeatedly flag daily rewards and streak systems as core retention levers because players log in to avoid losing progress, not out of genuine intrinsic desire to play.",!1)
B.R8=new A.a5(62459,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pf=new A.b4("forced_continuity","Forced Continuity","Free trials quietly roll into paid subscriptions with unclear reminders and buried cancellation flows, keeping you paying long after you meant to test a service once.","Subscription Platforms / 'Forced Continuity' Dark Pattern.","Use virtual cards or spending-limited cards for trials, set a calendar reminder for the exact end date, and cancel immediately after signup if you are unsure you will keep it.",B.cj,B.R8,"","Consumer-protection and UX audits single out forced continuity plus hard-to-find cancellation as among the most harmful subscription dark patterns.",!0)
B.QT=new A.a5(61254,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OV=new A.b4("prechecked_consent","Pre-Checked Consent","Cookie and privacy forms ship with non-essential tracking boxes already ticked so that doing nothing quietly opts you into extensive data sharing.","Cookie Banners and Consent UX.","Manually untick every non-essential category, use 'Reject All' where available, and rely on browsers or extensions that block trackers by default.",B.cj,B.QT,"","European data protection guidance explicitly states that pre-ticked boxes do not constitute valid consent, yet audits continue to find them in real-world cookie banners.",!1)
B.R3=new A.a5(62004,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OJ=new A.b4("nagging_prompts","Nagging Prompts","Apps repeatedly interrupt normal use with pop-ups asking you to enable notifications, share data, or rate the app, creating ambient pressure to eventually give in.","Mobile App Growth Teams / 'Nagging' Dark Pattern.","Dismiss prompts several times and choose 'Don\u2019t ask again' when offered, or revoke notification and tracking permissions at the OS level so the app cannot keep asking.",B.hC,B.R3,"","Dark-pattern taxonomies now treat nagging as its own category because repeated prompts measurably erode users\u2019 ability to make calm, autonomous choices.",!1)
B.Rb=new A.a5(62538,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Pb=new A.b4("appointment_mechanics","Appointment Mechanics","Timers and regenerating resources are tuned so that missing the 'harvest window' means losing progress or rewards, effectively scheduling your day around the app.","'Play by Appointment' Systems in Free-to-Play Games.","Turn off push alerts for timers, ignore 'optimal' harvest times, and favour games or tools that let you pause without penalty when life is busy.",B.f6,B.Rb,"","Analyses of dark patterns in games repeatedly identify time-gated events and regenerative timers as central to 'appointment gaming' that keeps players tethered to devices throughout the day.",!0)
B.QY=new A.a5(61784,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.OL=new A.b4("competitive_leaderboards","Competitive Leaderboards","Apps rank your performance against friends or strangers so that every workout, step count, or match becomes a visible status contest instead of a private goal.","Fitness and Social Apps Using Social Comparison Mechanics.","Hide or ignore public rankings, opt into effort-based or friends-only modes, or track progress privately with simple logs instead of public leaderboards.",B.dY,B.QY,"","Recent studies find leaderboard use can increase activity and app-checking frequency, but a meaningful minority also reports stress, guilt, or burnout from constant ranking.",!0)
